\begin_layout Standard
I have completed my report of 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Does the Military Train Men to be Violent Criminals? New Evidence from Australia
' Conscription Lotteries.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 While the topic is of interest, it has already been addressed by past research
 endeavors in which more convincing results were obtained.
 Using three distinct 2SLS specifications, the authors found no evidence
 that military training causes violent crime.
 Note that the point estimates were always negative, which contradicts some
 of the pertinent arguments expressed in Sections 2 and 3.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
I have several major criticisms concerning this paper.
 First, it failed to consider the heterogenous time effects of military
 training.
 It is not unreasonable to believe that the effects of military training
 may be more prominent during the earlier years of adulthood.
 Next, there is a potential selection issue because the outcome data only
 accounts for 77% of the Australian population.
 The remaining 23% of the population excluded from the data could the results.
 Moving on, the authors did not include state and/or region fixed in their
 specifications even though various states could have different criminal
 laws, political biases, court procedures, etc.
 Finally, no tests were conducted in order to verify that the binary conscriptio
n lottery ballot outcomes were randomly assigned.
 The identifying assumption (i.e., the exclusion restriction) and the entire
 analysis of the paper hinges on this fact.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Furthermore, there are several minor concerns and comments that I have specified
 in my report.
 The authors can provide the treatment effect estimates from the basic OLS
 regression, and they can show the actual reduced-form estimation results,
 instead of just the visual presentations.
 Above all, I would suggest expanding Sections 4 and 5 in order to provide
 a more thorough explanation of the utilized GLS method, as well as the
 construction of synthetic records for the first-stage data.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In my opinion, this paper does not add any pertinent revelations to the
 existing literature.
 As has been noted, the authors were unable to conclude that military training
 has a long-term effect on violent crime.
 Previous papers, on the other hand, have provided a more convincing analysis
 of this topic.
 My report also addresses the other issues present within the paper.
 Yet, the major criticisms just briefly discussed are the primary reasons
 I have for recommending rejection.
\end_layout

